    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Use b40eed9633274ff4 as the radix reference. Research reducing data movement through cache-sized bucket refinement, alternative digit widths and small-range cutoffs; account explicitly for buffer parity and final-copy costs. If OpenMP is already enabled, compare serial refinement with processing disjoint buckets in one bounded team on the four available cores. Preserve signed ordering, stable lower-digit passes, constant-digit skipping and allocation-failure fallback. Select by whole-program measurements, not sorting-only estimates.",
        "B": "Redirect low-level optimization toward reachable radix kernels rather than assuming the existing NEON partition accelerates A's winner. Investigate modest histogram unrolling, independent count accumulators and guarded ARM64 NEON digit extraction. Keep histogram updates collision-safe and scatter placement stable; SIMD lanes cannot be assumed to address different buckets. Measure initialization, histogram merging and temporary-storage costs. Retain the existing partition implementations as comparison alternatives, but require a whole-program improvement over the radix reference before promoting them.",
        "C": "Develop independently selectable generation and validation improvements from 51166684004c4cd7 and 76f3daa5b6724130. Preserve the exact MWC sequence, chunk boundaries, final global RNG state, original conversion/scaling behavior and sorted integer bytes. Retain exceptional skip-ahead fixed points and guarded sequential fallbacks. Investigate whether team startup and scan overhead remain worthwhile after sorting becomes much faster; parallelize only when supported by the unchanged build and permitted resources. Keep validation exact, including chunk-boundary comparisons, and leave the MD5 computation unchanged.",
        "G": "Start with the complete admissible commit 7e41988a96a49dc69d192c96b994586f14eff239, not reconstructed truncated diffs, and retain it as the control and fallback submission. Within the existing budget, measure radix plus C's generation change and radix plus C's validation change separately before considering both together. Evaluate later A/B kernels similarly, preserving an unmodified best candidate. Merge function-scoped changes carefully because all candidates modify src/quicksort.c. Do not add B's partition machinery expecting a benefit when radix bypasses it. Use unchanged correctness checks and whole-program timing to select each integration; reject regressions and never infer additive speedups. If no integration demonstrably wins, retain the standalone radix program.",
        "analysis": "The relevant workload is Problem B: generate the reproducible array, sort signed integers, validate and print its MD5. The supplied baseline is 69.091 ms. A's radix candidate leads at 17.396 ms, about 3.97x faster; block introsort takes 37.301 ms and the best NEON partition candidate takes 52.612 ms. C's generation and validation candidates take 68.150 and 68.786 ms respectively, showing small standalone gains but no established benefit when combined with radix. No integration measurements are supplied. Prioritize the radix winner, complementary reachable kernels and carefully measured surrounding-loop improvements. All objectives preserve every valid input and output behavior, including error handling and conditional checksum output; no expected-answer substitution or test-specific dispatch is allowed. Keep source work confined to the permitted optimization surface, without changing tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target or permissions."
      },
